The MPU-6050 supplies rich continuous data. The mechanical switches supply clean, low-effort
discrete events. Together they create layered awareness without overloading a single sensor or the
microcontroller.
3. Feasibility on Arduino Nano
Short answer: Yes — this combination is well within the Nano’s capabilities and is not
overloaded.
Resource picture:
• MPU-6050: Uses only the I2C bus (A4/A5). Modest sampling rates (20–50 Hz) are light work.
• SW-18015P: Simple digital switch. One digital pin + INPUT_PULLUP (or module DO pin).
Extremely low overhead.
• Tilt switch: Pure digital input. Negligible cost.
• The Nano still has ample pins, flash, and RAM left for millis-based timing, simple fusion, and
serial output to the ROS 2 pipeline.
Practical guidelines that keep the node reliable:
1. Debounce the mechanical switches (vibration switches in particular produce short, noisy
pulses).
