2. Sample the MPU moderately (20–50 Hz is plenty for droid awareness). Avoid unnecessary
high-rate streaming.
3. Consider interrupts for the vibration switches if you want very responsive shock detection
without constant polling.
4. Mount carefully — vibration switch sensitivity depends heavily on placement and orientation on
the chassis or dome.
4. How This Fits the Overall Architecture
Lesson Set 04 continues the same modular pattern established in Sets 01–03:
• Individual sensor nodes first (or a combined MCU sketch that still treats each sensor cleanly)
• Simple fusion into higher-level state
• Publication as ROS 2 topics that later nodes (OLED, sound, motion) can consume without
rewriting earlier work
Suggested topic direction (still evolving with the preferred naming style):
• /imu/data or simplified /orientation_state + /accel_events
• /shock_event (from SW-18015P)
• /tilt_state
• /fused_awareness (optional higher-level combination of motion + orientation + shock)
Because the earlier motion and ranging nodes remain independent, adding orientation awareness does
not force rework of the PIR/RCWL or distance pipelines. This is intentional and supports the long-term
goal of reusable, teachable modules.
5. Parts Already on Hand
This lesson set is designed around parts already available in the lab:
• MPU-6050 modules
• SW-18015P shock/vibration switches (one or two recommended)
• Tilt switch
• Arduino Nano as the local MCU (consistent with earlier sensor nodes)
The same millis-based sketch style used in the PIR/RCWL and combined distance sketches will be
extended here so students see continuity rather than a completely new coding pattern.
6. Learning Outcomes
By the end of Lesson Set 04 a student should be able to:
1. Wire and read an MPU-6050 on an Arduino Nano and extract useful orientation/acceleration
information.
2. Add one or two SW-18015P vibration switches and a tilt switch, handle debouncing, and treat
them as clean digital events.
3. Publish the resulting data in a form that fits the existing ROS 2 sensor pipeline.
4. Explain why continuous IMU data and discrete mechanical switches are complementary rather
than redundant.
5. Design simple reactive behaviors (sound, state change, later motion) driven by orientation or
shock events.
